---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../../../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import PageShell from '../../../../../components/PageShell.astro';
import { unit9Sessions } from '../../../../../data/generalEnglishUnit9';
import { withBase } from '../../../../../utils/paths';
const title = 'Unit 9 teaching guide · General English I';
const description = 'Preparation, 75-minute teaching sequences, feedback guidance and original listening alternatives for Unit 9.';
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description}>
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main-content" class="bg-page"><PageShell accentBar>
    <article class="guide">
      <h1>Unit 9 teaching guide</h1>
      <p>Four 75-minute lessons for first-year undergraduates, using <em>Perspectives</em> Pre-Intermediate. The sequence covers 9A–9E selectively and connects the language to decisions students can make now.</p>
      <h2>Before teaching</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Open the e-book at pp.104–115. Session 1 uses the original worker clues in its print notes. For Session 3, check Audio 60, Audio 56 and short extracts from Videos 9.2–9.4. Audio and textbook video are played from the e-book, not hosted on this site.</li>
        <li>Session 4 uses 9E pp.114–115 for skills conversations and an enquiry email.</li>
        <li>Use the printable practice sheets, notebooks or browser response spaces. No student writing is uploaded or submitted by these pages. Each session has a separate browser save key and a text download.</li>
        <li>Session 4 uses an original enquiry email model.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Session 1: two hands-on tasks</h2>
      <p>Print the <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-1/student-handout.pdf')}>four-page student handout</a>, <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-1/group-mats-and-cards.pdf')}>mats and cut-out cards</a> and <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-1/teaching-notes.pdf')}>two-page teaching notes</a>. The notes specify copy quantities, physical card sizes, worker scripts, answers and the 75-minute sequence.</p>
      <p>Start with the vocabulary and sentence frames, then place cards and justify matches. Each student gives a job explanation. Next, model earlier and later events, practise the verb patterns, arrange five event cards on a personal timeline and prepare a story. Every student shares. Language banks remain available throughout.</p>
      <h2>Session 2: vocabulary, viewpoint, tone and debate</h2>
      <p>Use the <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-2/language-bank.pdf')}>60-phrase bank</a>, <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-2/student-handout.pdf')}>one-page student handout</a> and <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-2/teaching-notes.pdf')}>teaching notes</a>. For a physical matching activity, print the <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-2/phrase-meaning-cards.pdf')}>English–Chinese cards</a>: four sets of 15 pairs. Give one card to each student and ask them to find their partner. Twelve starred chunks form the active set. The phrase activity has three multiple-choice formats: meaning, missing preposition and use in a sentence. Students choose an answer, check it, then continue. Use the 12 core phrases in class; all 60 are available for review.</p>
      <p>The six reading cards appear together on each HTML grouping board. Students drag cards into groups, or select a card and choose a destination. First group writers by position and explain a reason. Then regroup by tone using exact wording. Check grouping shows feedback; teacher sorting stays in the separate Teacher controls section. Compare writers with the same position but different tone. No reading cut-outs are required. End with a debate: every student gives an opening and a response, using a clear claim, concrete support and an explanation of the connection.</p>
      <h2>Session 3: gossip, interviews and 9D</h2>
      <p>Print the <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-3/student-handout.pdf')}>two-page 9C handout</a> double-sided. It contains Unit 1, 2, 5 and 9 vocabulary, interview/report examples and role rotations. Use the <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-3/teaching-notes.pdf')}>one-page teaching plan</a> for timings and checks.</p>
      <p>Start with the influencer rumour. Keep slides 6–10 for reported speech and question pronunciation. Two students interview; the others report. Rotate every three minutes in groups of four, or every four minutes in groups of three. Each student shares two reports checked by the speaker.</p>
      <p>Use textbook pp.112–113 directly for 9D: Audio 56 and Videos 9.2–9.4. End with the short reported-speech response on the handout.</p>
      <h2>Session 4: speaking to writing</h2>
      <p>Print the <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-4/student-handout.pdf')}>two-page handout</a> double-sided and use the <a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/session-4/teaching-notes.pdf')}>one-page teaching plan</a>. Reuse skills and experience from Session 1, chunks and supported reasons from Session 2, and questions and accurate reports from Session 3.</p>
      <p>Students rotate through a skills-advice conversation, choose a fictional workshop and identify three missing details. They write a 90–120-word enquiry with at least two indirect questions, act as a recipient to give feedback, then revise. Every student produces a supported spoken choice and a revised email.</p>
      <h2>Teaching routine</h2>
      <p>Give individual thinking time, let students rehearse in pairs, create a reason to communicate, then offer feedback before a second attempt. Collect an individual response at the end. Brief Chinese planning can support ideas; the final spoken or written product should use accessible English.</p>
      <p>Offer a vocabulary bank and sentence openings to students who need support. Confident students can add a follow-up question, an alternative interpretation or an unexpected constraint. Students may choose real or imagined experiences.</p>
      {unit9Sessions.map(session => { let elapsed=0; return <section>
        <h2>{session.number}. {session.title.en}</h2><p>{session.outcome.en}</p>
        <table><thead><tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Activity</th></tr></thead><tbody>{session.slides.map(slide => { const start=elapsed; elapsed+=slide.minutes; return <tr><td>{start}–{elapsed}</td><td><a href={withBase(`/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9/${session.slug}#slide-${session.slides.indexOf(slide)+1}`)}>{slide.title.en}</a>{slide.ref && <small>{slide.ref}</small>}</td></tr>; })}</tbody></table>
      </section>; })}
      <h2>What to check</h2>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Lesson</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Feedback priority</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>Individual job explanation and five-event story</td><td>Specific reason; accurate event order and had + past participle.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>Phrase use, tone judgements and revised debate argument</td><td>Viewpoint and tone are distinguished; examples support a precise claim; at least three chunks fit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>Verified report and individual exit response</td><td>Information, certainty and conditions survive; pronouns and tense fit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>Supported spoken choice and revised enquiry</td><td>Advice fits the experience; three useful questions, at least two indirect; clear purpose and accurate word order.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <p>Use these as formative checks. The website does not assign grades. During group work, note a few examples for whole-class feedback and ask every student to revise one sentence. Avoid correcting every error during a speaking turn.</p>
      <h2>Content boundaries</h2>
      <p>Past perfect clarifies an earlier event viewed from a past moment; it is not compulsory whenever <em>before</em> or <em>after</em> appears. Reported speech practice starts with common backshift, while allowing contextually valid present forms for information that remains true. Reported questions are not assessed. Question intonation patterns are useful tendencies, not universal rules.</p>
      <p>All workshop options are fictional classroom situations. The email model is original. Reported statements pass on information; indirect questions request it.</p>
      <p><a href={withBase('/teaching/courses/general-english/unit-9')}>← Unit 9 overview</a></p>
    </article>
  </PageShell></main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
<style>
.guide { max-width:78ch; } h1 { font:600 2.5rem/1.1 'Source Serif 4',serif; } h2 { font-size:1.35rem; font-weight:600; margin:2rem 0 .7rem; }
p,li { line-height:1.65; } p { margin:.9rem 0; } ul,ol { padding-left:1.5rem; list-style:revert; } li + li { margin-top:.5rem; }
table { width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:1rem 0; } td,th { text-align:left; vertical-align:top; border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-rule); padding:.65rem; } th:first-child { width:6rem; } small { display:block; margin-top:.3rem; }
details { border-block-start:1px solid var(--color-rule); padding:1rem 0; } summary { cursor:pointer; min-height:44px; font-weight:600; } blockquote { border-left:3px solid var(--color-rule); padding-left:1rem; }
@media (max-width:600px) { td,th { padding:.45rem; font-size:.95rem; } }
</style>
